1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,962
Welcome to lesson fifteen: NetworkPolicies and Pod security.

2
00:00:05,062 --> 00:00:12,576
The goal is to limit the damage if something is compromised. Pods should only talk to what they need, and run with only the privileges they need.

3
00:00:13,926 --> 00:00:19,840
By default, Kubernetes networking is flat. Every Pod can reach every other Pod, in every namespace.

4
00:00:19,940 --> 00:00:29,326
So if an attacker exploits a bug in your public web Pod, they can connect straight to the database, or to services in other namespaces, or download tools from the internet.

5
00:00:29,426 --> 00:00:33,420
NetworkPolicies are firewall rules for Pods that stop this.

6
00:00:34,770 --> 00:00:37,500
A few rules explain almost everything.

7
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:43,610
You need a network plugin that enforces policies. Without one, they're accepted and silently ignored.

8
00:00:43,710 --> 00:00:52,472
A policy selects Pods. Once a Pod is selected for incoming or outgoing traffic, only explicitly allowed traffic passes in that direction.

9
00:00:52,572 --> 00:01:00,150
Policies add up, replies are allowed automatically, and they work only with IPs and ports, not paths or domain names.

10
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:05,878
Here's a policy. It protects the api Pods in the shop namespace.

11
00:01:05,978 --> 00:01:12,692
They accept traffic only from Pods labeled web, in the same namespace, only on TCP port eighty eighty.

12
00:01:12,792 --> 00:01:16,194
Note that the port is the Pod's own port, not the Service port.

13
00:01:17,544 --> 00:01:20,290
This is the most common NetworkPolicy bug.

14
00:01:20,390 --> 00:01:28,240
A namespace selector and a Pod selector in one list item are combined with AND: only Prometheus Pods inside the monitoring namespace.

15
00:01:28,340 --> 00:01:38,302
Put them in two separate items, with one extra dash, and they're combined with OR: any Pod in monitoring, or Prometheus Pods in this namespace. That's much more permissive.

16
00:01:39,652 --> 00:01:43,150
Start every namespace with a default deny for both directions.

17
00:01:43,250 --> 00:01:51,900
Then allow DNS. If you forget this after denying outgoing traffic, every name lookup fails. Many AI-generated policies forget it.

18
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:59,290
Then allow each app-to-app path, on both sides, allow your Ingress controller, and allow specific external ranges.

19
00:02:00,640 --> 00:02:08,490
Now Pod security. A container is a process on the node, and some settings weaken its isolation. The security context controls them.

20
00:02:08,590 --> 00:02:22,680
Run as a non-root user. Block privilege escalation. Make the root filesystem read-only, with a writable temp folder. Drop all Linux capabilities. And use the runtime's default seccomp profile, which blocks dangerous system calls.

21
00:02:24,030 --> 00:02:27,272
Some settings effectively give root access to the node.

22
00:02:27,372 --> 00:02:33,590
Privileged mode, host namespaces, and mounting sensitive host paths like the container runtime's socket.

23
00:02:33,690 --> 00:02:39,380
Only node-level system components should ever need these. Never use them to fix a permission problem.

24
00:02:40,730 --> 00:02:49,188
Kubernetes defines three Pod Security Standards: privileged, baseline, and restricted. Restricted is the target for app namespaces.

25
00:02:49,288 --> 00:02:56,114
Pod Security Admission enforces them with namespace labels, in three modes: enforce, warn and audit.

26
00:02:56,214 --> 00:03:06,624
Roll out with warn and audit first. And note that enforcement checks Pods: a Deployment is accepted, but its ReplicaSet can't create Pods, so look for errors there.

27
00:03:06,724 --> 00:03:12,862
Pod Security Policy was removed in version one twenty-five. Any answer that suggests it is outdated.

28
00:03:14,212 --> 00:03:17,918
For your organization's own rules, use a policy engine.

29
00:03:18,018 --> 00:03:26,348
The built-in ValidatingAdmissionPolicy uses CEL expressions, with no extra software. Kyverno and OPA Gatekeeper offer more features.

30
00:03:26,448 --> 00:03:35,354
Typical rules: only allow images from your registry, block the latest tag, require labels and limits, and verify image signatures.

31
00:03:36,704 --> 00:03:44,890
In the lab, you'll lock down a three-tier app, so web can reach api, api can reach the database, but web can't reach the database.

32
00:03:44,990 --> 00:03:50,728
You'll fall into the AND or OR trap and fix it, break DNS with an egress deny and fix that too.

33
00:03:50,828 --> 00:03:55,494
And you'll enforce the restricted standard and run a hardened, non-root nginx.

34
00:03:56,844 --> 00:03:57,990
Let's recap.

35
00:03:58,090 --> 00:04:03,156
The network is flat until you add policies, and policies need a supporting network plugin.

36
00:04:03,256 --> 00:04:10,226
Start with default deny, allow DNS, then allow each path on both sides. Watch the AND or OR dash.

37
00:04:10,326 --> 00:04:14,768
Harden containers, and enforce the restricted standard on app namespaces.

38
00:04:14,868 --> 00:04:19,246
Next, you'll package and manage manifests with Helm and Kustomize.
